/-! # Affine neighbourhoods defined by sections of invertible modules

Source: Stacks Project, Divisors, Tag 0GML, lemma-regular-ample-family, specialized
from affine diagonal to separated schemes; Morphisms, Tag 0FXR,
definition-family-ample-invertible-modules
(blueprint: lem-sfb-regular-scheme-affine-line-section-cover).
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u

namespace AlgebraicGeometry

/-- Every point of a quasi-compact regular separated scheme has an affine neighbourhood
which is the nonvanishing locus of a global section of an invertible module. The module
may depend on the point.
Source: Stacks Project, Divisors, Tag 0GML, lemma-regular-ample-family, and Morphisms,
Tag 0FXR, definition-family-ample-invertible-modules, absorbing the positive tensor power
into the chosen module (blueprint: lem-sfb-regular-scheme-affine-line-section-cover).

Proof:
1. By def-weil-regular-scheme and compactness, X is Noetherian. Apply clauses (1)-(2) of rem-pc-
regular-normal to decompose it into finitely many disjoint clopen normal integral components.
Let Y contain x and let Z be its complement. The closed Y is compact (extend an open cover by
Z); as an open subscheme it is locally Noetherian (pqf-def-locnoeth) with the same regular
stalks. Its diagonal is a base change of that of X, hence a closed immersion by lem-weil-pbc-
closed-immersion-base-change. Thus Y is integral, Noetherian, regular and separated.

2. Choose an affine neighbourhood U of x in Y by lem-weil-pbc-affine-opens-basis. It is nonempty
and dense by irreducibility (def-pc-integral-scheme). By lem-sc-noetherian-closed-subset-finite-
components the boundary has finitely many irreducible components E_i. Give them their reduced
closed subscheme structures by lem-weil-pbc-reduced-induced-closed-subscheme; they are integral,
and lem-weil-pbc-scheme-sober gives their generic points xi_i. An empty boundary gives zero
components.

3. By lem-sfb-affine-open-boundary-codimension-one, the stalk at each xi_i has dimension one
(def-weil-pbc-krull-dimension-ring). Outside E_i its ideal stalk is generated by 1. At y in E_i,
choose an affine chart Spec R and let q and p be the primes of xi_i and y. Reducedness
identifies the ideal on the chart with q. By lem-weil-pbc-affine-open-stalk-localization and
lem-weil-pbc-spec-tilde-stalk-localization, its stalk is q R_p, and the stalk at xi_i is R_q
(def-weil-pbc-localization-at-prime). Prime correspondence shows q R_p has height one since dim
R_q = 1. The regular local ring R_p is a Noetherian UFD by lem-trf-regular-local-ufd-boundary-
factorization with r = 0. By lem-trf-ufd-height-one its nonzero prime q R_p is principal. Its
nonzero generator is a nonzerodivisor in the domain R_p.

4. Use lem-weil-pbc-product-ideal-closed-subscheme-union to multiply the finitely many boundary
ideals (def-sbc-ideal-sheaf-powers); the resulting closed subscheme D has support the boundary.
For no components use the unit ideal and empty D. Localization commutes with finite ideal
products: fractions in a finite sum of products have a common denominator, and conversely a
fraction with numerator in the product splits into products of fractions. Each stalk ideal is
therefore generated by the product of the generators in step 3. Successive cancellation makes
this product a nonzerodivisor; the empty product is 1. Apply the scheme clause of lem-sfb-stalk-
nonzerodivisor-cartier-spreading to get the effective Cartier condition (def-nagata-effective-
cartier-ideal). Thus Y minus D is U.

5. By lem-pc-divisor-line-bundle-invertible, L = O_Y(D), the dual of the ideal, is invertible.
By lem-sc-canonical-section-zero-scheme its canonical section s has zero scheme D. In a local
frame write s = a; its zero ideal is (a) by def-pc-zero-scheme. Membership in D means that a
lies in the maximal ideal. Thus the nonvanishing locus of def-weil-pbc-ample-invertible-module
is precisely U.

6. On X = Y disjoint union Z define A(V) = L(V intersect Y) times O_Z(V intersect Z), with
restrictions and O_X-actions componentwise. The sheaf axiom holds in both factors. Local frames
of L and O_Z cover X, so A is invertible (def-sc-invertible-module). The section t = (s, 0) is
nonvanishing exactly on U by its stalks on the two components. It gives the required affine open
containing x. If X is empty there is no x to check.
-/
theorem exists_affine_nonvanishingLocus_of_isRegularScheme
    (X : Scheme.{u}) [CompactSpace X] (hX : IsRegularScheme X) [X.IsSeparated]
    (x : X) :
    ∃ A : X.Modules, A.IsInvertible ∧
      ∃ t : A.val.presheaf.obj (Opposite.op ⊤), ∃ U : X.Opens,
        x ∈ U ∧ IsAffineOpen U ∧ (U : Set X) = A.nonvanishingLocus t := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry
